% ============================================================================
% circuit-parts — the basic parts of a simple electric circuit and the role /
% function its source states each one performs (adj-facts-stdlib, PHYSICS).
% ============================================================================
% One of the first big ideas of electricity, and a rung a PHYSICS agent reasons
% UP from: a simple circuit is built out of a handful of basic parts, and each
% part does ONE characteristic job. The battery provides the power; the wire
% carries the current; the switch opens or closes the loop; the lamp turns the
% electricity into light; the resistor slows the current down. Before you can
% reason about WHY a bulb lights (or doesn't), you must first know what each
% part is FOR. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? circuit_part_role(switch, $Role)      % opens_or_closes
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `circuit_part_role(part, role)` carrying the table's citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the role
% WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a word
% that is not one of these basic parts (it never invents a role).
%
% Each basic circuit part and the role / function the source states for it, as
% a little truth table:
%
%     part       role the source states           a beginner's cue
%     --------   ----------------------------      -------------------------------
%     battery    provides_dc_power                 the power source of the circuit
%     wire       carries_current                   the conductor current flows down
%     switch     opens_or_closes                   opens or closes the loop (on/off)
%     lamp       converts_to_light                 turns electricity into light
%     resistor   slows_current                     slows the flow of electricity
%     motor      converts_to_motion                turns electricity into rotation
%     led        gives_off_light                   gives off light when current flows
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a role and recall the part that does it:
%
%     ? circuit_part_role($Part, carries_current)   % wire
%
% *** THE LOCATOR THIS TABLE SHIPPED NAMED A FILE THAT NEVER EXISTED, and
% that is worth recording rather than quietly fixing. *** It read
% `.../circuit_basics_and_components.pdf`. That 404s, and the Wayback CDX
% index holds NO capture of it at any time — while TWO separate files in the
% same folder, `circuit_basics.pdf` and `circuit_components.pdf`, both
% returned 200 and are archived. The shipped name is those two run together.
% A dead link is a page that went away; this was an address that was never
% right, and #15199 recorded it as the former.
%
% THE QUOTE WAS SOUND ALL ALONG. The shipped span occurs in
% `circuit_components.pdf`, which defines all seven parts, so the sentence
% really was taken from a real document and only the address was wrong.
%
% THE LOCATOR NOW POINTS AT A FETCHABLE CAPTURE of that correct file. The
% live URL is 404 today (the whole folder is gone), so pointing at it would
% restore a correct-but-unfetchable address; the capture was verified at
% HTTP 200, 449,715 bytes. The capture's Memento response headers name the
% original URL (`Link: <...circuit_components.pdf>; rel="original"`) and pin
% the date (`Memento-Datetime: Thu, 17 Nov 2022 21:26:30 GMT`). An earlier
% wording here said the capture "names the original URL inside it" — it does
% not; the string `k12maker` occurs zero times in the captured bytes, and the
% headers are what carry that. This is an address a reader can open — NOT a
% content hash, and nothing here claims more than `source_labeled`.
%
% "VERBATIM" IS WEAKER HERE THAN FOR THIS CASCADE'S HTML TABLES, and saying
% so is the point: the source is a PDF whose text layout wraps sentences
% across lines, so each span below matches only after WHITESPACE COLLAPSING.
% Measured 2026-09-14 against the captured file: each of the seven occurs
% EXACTLY ONCE, none names another row's key, and a fabricated control
% sentence is reported absent by the same comparison.
%
% A word that is NOT one of these basic parts — `capacitor`, `transistor`,
% `rock` — is deliberately NOT a row, so a circuit-part recall ABSTAINS on it
% rather than inventing a role. That honest-abstention property is what makes
% the table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the part→role pairs
% it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `role` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be a
% role / function the source EXPLICITLY states for that part — never one the
% source does not support. Where the everyday word differs from the source's
% word (the source says the lamp "converts electrical energy into light", not
% "glows"; the resistor "slows the flow of electricity", not "resists"), the
% atom uses the source's own word, so every value stays literally checkable
% against the quoted span.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every part→role pair below
% was read out of one fetched MIT K-12 Maker "Circuit Components" teaching
% handout, and any pair whose role could not be verified there would have been
% dropped).
%
% THAT TITLE WAS ALSO CONFLATED, and it survived the first pass of this fix.
% It read "Circuit Basics and Components" — a title that occurs ZERO times in
% either PDF. `circuit_components.pdf` is titled "Circuit Components" and
% `circuit_basics.pdf` is "Circuit Basics". The same run-together mistake as
% the filename, thirty-five lines above the paragraph that corrects the
% filename, found only because a reviewer grepped the prose for the twin of
% the URL I had already fixed.
%
% Each pair, with the span that states it (whitespace-collapsed — see above):
%
%   battery → provides_dc_power
%     "Battery: A device that converts chemical energy into electrical energy
%      and provides direct current (DC) power."
%
%   wire → carries_current
%     "Conductor: Any material, usually metal, that carries an electrical
%      current, such as wire, alligator clips, or copper tape."
%
%   switch → opens_or_closes
%     "The handle moves side to side to open or close the circuit."
%     (This entry used to open with "This switch serves as an On/Off switch in
%      a circuit." — a sentence that occurs ZERO times in either PDF, inside a
%      block headed "the verbatim span". The source's own wording is
%      ungrammatical: "Toggle SPST switches are often serve as an On/Off
%      switches in a circuit." Somewhere it was silently tidied into something
%      that reads well and was never written. The SHIPPED ROW was always the
%      second sentence, which is verbatim; only this listing overstated.)
%
%   lamp → converts_to_light
%     "Lamp: This traditional device converts electrical energy into light (and
%      lots of heat) using a resistive filament."
%
%   resistor → slows_current
%     "Resistor: Electronic component that slows the flow of electricity."
%
%   motor → converts_to_motion
%     "There are many types of electric motors, but all convert electrical
%      energy into rotary motion of an output shaft."
%
%   led → gives_off_light
%     "LED: Light Emitting Diodes are components that give off light when
%      current goes through in the correct direction, as indicated by the
%      “arrow” in the symbol."
%
% All spans are the same primary source, and it is `circuit_components.pdf`
% — NOT the conflated `circuit_basics_and_components.pdf` this listing used
% to name, which never existed:
%   https://k12maker.mit.edu/uploads/9/7/5/8/97583140/circuit_components.pdf
%   (MIT K-12 Maker — "Circuit Components")
% cited through the capture at the `locator` below, because the live address
% is 404 today.
%
% EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SPAN as its primary warrant (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e).
% This paragraph used to read "An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance
% envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/`trust` below hold the single cleanest
% span ... Every OTHER row's verbatim span is listed above" — true of the old
% shape, false of this one, and an auditor reading top-down met it before
% reaching the table. THE SPANS IN THE TABLE ARE THE AUTHORITY; this listing
% is commentary, and where the two differ the table wins.
%
% The source is a university (MIT) STEM-outreach teaching handout — a
% teaching summary, not a primary standards reference — so `trust consensus`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table circuit_part_role {
    columns part, role

    % Each row carries the sentence that DEFINES ITS OWN PART. Until
    % issue #14986 was applied here, the BATTERY sentence was the
    % envelope `source` and therefore the warrant for all seven rows, so
    % `? circuit_part_role(resistor, $R)` came back proved by a sentence
    % about batteries. Six of the seven were in that position.
    %
    % The WIRE row's span defines `Conductor` and names wire as an
    % example of one -- that is the page's own wording, and the row is
    % `wire` because that is the word a beginner brings. Measured: each
    % of the seven spans occurs EXACTLY ONCE in the document and names
    % no other row's key.
    row (battery, provides_dc_power) {
        source "Battery: A device that converts chemical energy into electrical energy and provides direct current (DC) power."
    }
    row (wire, carries_current) {
        source "Conductor: Any material, usually metal, that carries an electrical current, such as wire, alligator clips, or copper tape."
    }
    row (switch, opens_or_closes) {
        source "The handle moves side to side to open or close the circuit."
    }
    row (lamp, converts_to_light) {
        source "Lamp: This traditional device converts electrical energy into light (and lots of heat) using a resistive filament."
    }
    row (resistor, slows_current) {
        source "Resistor: Electronic component that slows the flow of electricity."
    }
    row (motor, converts_to_motion) {
        source "Motor: There are many types of electric motors, but all convert electrical energy into rotary motion of an output shaft."
    }
    row (led, gives_off_light) {
        source "LED: Light Emitting Diodes are components that give off light when current goes through in the correct direction, as indicated by the “arrow” in the symbol."
    }

    % THE ENVELOPE KEEPS THE BATTERY SPAN, disclosed rather than dressed
    % up as framing: this document is a definition table with no prose
    % framing it, so there is no sentence that covers the whole relation.
    % An envelope `source` is REQUIRED, so the span stays; every row
    % overrides it, so it reaches no answer, and `battery` carries its
    % own copy.
    source "Battery: A device that converts chemical energy into electrical energy and provides direct current (DC) power."
    locator "https://web.archive.org/web/20221117212630if_/https://k12maker.mit.edu/uploads/9/7/5/8/97583140/circuit_components.pdf"
    trust consensus
}
